// A declared `op` is what lets a `rule` become a cell.
//
// The description layer records an operation's arity as a bridge diagram; the
// cell layer reads that arity to decide whether it can hold the operation at
// all. `add` reads two inputs and produces one result, which is exactly the
// shape a cut against the operation frame `add(n; $ret)` can carry — so both
// rules below elaborate.
//
// What lands in the store is more than the two rules. Every constructor also
// contributes its return-side frame's defining cell (`Succ⁻(β)` reduces to
// `Succ(v)` handed to `β`), which is what makes the second rule's flattened
// right-hand side reduce. Four cells: two frames and two rules.
data NatAdd {
  Zero,
  Succ(n: NatAdd),
  oper add(m: NatAdd, n: NatAdd) -> NatAdd,
  rule add(Zero, n) ==> n,
  rule add(Succ(m), n) ==> Succ(add(m, n))
}

//@ mode: desc
//@ expect-desc-rules: 2
//@ expect-desc-store-cells: 4
